<div style="background-color: #1a5276; color: white; padding: 20px; text-align: center; border-radius: 8px;">
    <h1 style="margin: 0;">Lesson Planner – Theme # You Can Save Water By... – Lesson # 1</h1>
    <p style="margin: 5px 0 0 0;">Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 1 of 5</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Listening</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Grade:</b> 8th Grade (CEFR: A2.3)</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Scenario:</b> The Environment and Recycling</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Theme:</b> You Can Save Water By...</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Listening: Receptive: Infer implicit information when listening to current events. Interactive: Respond to questions in class discussions.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Listening: Can infer simple details from short talks about sustainable living by recognizing key cause-effect relationships (e.g., "What happens if we waste water?" "We have less to drink.")
            Can respond to questions about recycling during class discussions, demonstrating understanding of main ideas (e.g., "What should we recycle?" "Cans, paper, cardboard, batteries, and plastic containers.")
            Can listen to peers discussing water conservation, showing comprehension by recognizing specific suggestions (e.g., "Use less water when washing dishes.")</p>
        </td>
    </tr>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 20px;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>
<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tr style="background-color: #f2f2f2;">
        <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 33%; color: #1a5276;">Stage & Description</th>
        <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 33%; color: #1a5276;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
        <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 33%; color: #1a5276;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
    </tr>
    <tr>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task (Engagement, Modeling and Clarification)</b></p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">This stage aims to engage students, activate prior knowledge, and introduce the theme and target vocabulary in a meaningful context.</p>
        </td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher displays various pictures on the board: a running tap, someone brushing teeth with the water off, a full garbage can, a recycling bin, a polluted river, and a clean park. The teacher asks, "What do you see in these pictures? Are they good or bad for our planet?" Students share initial thoughts, linking to the theme "You Can Save Water By..." and "The Environment and Recycling."</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher introduces key vocabulary words from the images: <i>waste</i>, <i>save</i>, <i>recycle</i>, <i>environment</i>, <i>pollution</i>, <i>planet</i>. The teacher says each word clearly and provides a simple definition or action. For instance, "When water runs and you don't use it, you <b>waste</b> water." "When you turn off the tap, you <b>save</b> water." The teacher models the pronunciation of past tense '-ed' endings, contrasting present and past: "We <i>watch</i> a video. Yesterday, we <i>watched</i> a video." (/t/) "We <i>save</i> water. Last week, we <i>saved</i> water." (/d/) "We <i>want</i> a cleaner planet. We <i>wanted</i> to clean the park." (/ɪd/). This prepares students to listen for these sounds.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b> The teacher checks understanding with questions like:</p>
            <ul>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"If I throw plastic bottles in the normal trash, am I <b>recycling</b> them or creating <b>waste</b>?" (Expected answer: "Creating waste.")</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Is it good for our <b>environment</b> to <b>save</b> water?" (Expected answer: "Yes, it is good.")</li>
            </ul>
        </td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">___ min</p>
        </td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 2 — Presentation</b></p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Students are presented with authentic input relevant to the scenario and theme, focusing on listening comprehension and new language structures.</p>
        </td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> A short audio clip (approx. 1.5 - 2 minutes) from a simulated podcast or public service announcement about water conservation tips and the importance of recycling. The audio features common phrases such as "If we waste water, there will be less for everyone," "You should turn off the tap when brushing your teeth," and "In the past, people didn't recycle that much. Today people are more aware."</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Infer implicit information when listening to current events. Respond to questions in class discussions.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b></p>
            <ul>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>First listening (Gist):</b> The teacher plays the audio once. Students listen for the main idea. "What is the audio mainly about: saving money, saving water, or having fun?"</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Second listening (True/False):</b> The teacher plays the audio again. Students listen and decide if the following statements are True (T) or False (F):
                    <ul style="margin-top: 5px;">
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 2px; text-align: justify;">a. You should leave the tap running when washing dishes. (F)</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 2px; text-align: justify;">b. Recycling helps to protect our environment. (T)</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 2px; text-align: justify;">c. If we save water, there will be more for the future. (T)</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 2px; text-align: justify;">d. In the past, people recycled more often than today. (F)</li>
                    </ul>
                </li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Third listening (Information matching):</b> Students listen one more time and match the problem with the suggestion mentioned in the audio (e.g., Problem: "Water waste when showering." Suggestion: "Take shorter showers.").</li>
            </ul>
        </td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">___ min</p>
        </td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</b></p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">This stage focuses on controlled and semi-controlled practice of the target language (grammar, vocabulary, pronunciation) through listening activities.</p>
        </td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Listening for specific information; using Past simple vs. present practices, First conditional, Modal 'should'. Vocabulary: <i>recycle</i>, <i>waste</i>, <i>environment</i>, <i>pollution</i>, <i>energy</i>, <i>planet</i>, <i>resource</i>, <i>reduce</i>, <i>reuse</i>, <i>clean</i>, <i>save</i>, <i>protect</i>.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b></p>
            <ul>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">1. <b>Listening for Gaps:</b> The teacher provides a partial transcript of the audio with blanks for target vocabulary (e.g., <i>waste, save, recycle, protect</i>) and grammar structures (e.g., "If we ___ water, there will be ___ for everyone." "You ___ turn off the tap."). Students listen to the audio again and fill in the missing words individually. The teacher then plays it one more time for students to check their answers, explicitly highlighting the target words and structures.</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">2. <b>Listen and Transform:</b> The teacher reads sentences. Students listen and then transform them using the target grammar.
                    <ul style="margin-top: 5px;">
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 2px; text-align: justify;">a. Teacher: "People used to throw all their trash together." Student response (Past vs. Present): "In the past, people didn't recycle that much. Today people are more aware of the importance of recycling."</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 2px; text-align: justify;">b. Teacher: "We don't close the water tap completely." Student response (First Conditional): "If we waste water, there will be less for everyone."</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 2px; text-align: justify;">c. Teacher: "I always leave the lights on when I leave a room." Student response (Modal 'should'): "You should turn off the lights to save energy."</li>
                    </ul>
                    The teacher pays attention to pronunciation of '-ed' endings and corrects as needed.
                </li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">3. <b>Pair Discussion - Listen and Agree/Disagree:</b> Students work in pairs. The teacher provides a list of statements related to water conservation and recycling (e.g., "We should only recycle paper and plastic bottles." "It's okay to let the water run while soaping dishes."). Students listen to each statement and discuss whether they agree or disagree, explaining why. They are encouraged to use "You should...", "If we...", "In the past...", and new vocabulary like <i>protect the environment</i>, <i>reduce pollution</i>. This promotes interactive listening and responding.</li>
            </ul>
        </td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">___ min</p>
        </td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</b></p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Students apply the learned language in a more realistic and communicative listening-focused task, demonstrating fluency and interaction.</p>
        </td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task format:</b> Group Problem-Solving Discussion: "Eco-Friendly Advice Column"</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b> Students work in small groups (3-4). The teacher provides each group with 2-3 "letters" from fictional people describing common environmental problems related to water waste or lack of recycling (e.g., "Dear Eco-Expert, My family leaves the tap running while brushing teeth, and we throw all our trash together. What should we do?").</p>
            <ul>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Each group listens to each problem letter read aloud by one member.</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students collaboratively discuss solutions and advice, focusing on listening to each other's suggestions and responding.</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">They must use the target grammar (e.g., "You <b>should</b> explain to your family the importance of saving water." "<b>If</b> we don't <b>recycle</b> plastic, it <b>will pollute</b> our <b>environment</b>." "<b>In the past</b>, people didn't know much, but <b>today</b> we can <b>protect</b> our <b>planet</b>.") and vocabulary (<i>recycle, waste, environment, pollution, save, protect, energy, resource</i>).</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">After discussing, each group chooses one letter and prepares to present their advice to the class. The emphasis is on active listening within the group and clear articulation of solutions.</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Sociolinguistic: Students collaborate in problem-solving group sessions to propose solutions to environmental issues.</li>
            </ul>
        </td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">___ min</p>
        </td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</b></p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">This stage focuses on formative assessment, allowing students to demonstrate their listening comprehension and the ability to respond using the target language.</p>
        </td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative assessment strategy:</b> Teacher Observation Checklist during group discussions and a quick "Listen and Answer" activity.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> The teacher plays a short, new audio clip (e.g., a short message about a local community's initiative to save water). After listening, students respond orally to two comprehension questions asked by the teacher, demonstrating their ability to infer simple details and recognize cause-effect. Example questions: "What is the community trying to do?" "If everyone participates, what will be the effect?" The teacher notes responses on a checklist.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria:</b></p>
            <ul>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Intelligible pronunciation, especially of "-ed" endings in past simple verbs.</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Appropriate use of target vocabulary (<i>recycle, waste, environment, save, protect, pollution, energy</i>).</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Clarity in expressing advice ("You should...") and cause-effect ("If... there will be...").</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Demonstrates understanding of main ideas and details from the audio clips and peer discussions.</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Collaborates actively and responds effectively in group problem-solving.</li>
            </ul>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students answer the following questions on a small slip of paper:</p>
            <ul>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">1. "What is one new thing I learned today about saving water or recycling?"</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">2. "What is one thing I understood clearly when listening to the audio or my classmates?"</li>
            </ul>
        </td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">___ min</p>
        </td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 6 — Reflection</b></p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">This final stage encourages both student and teacher to reflect on the learning process and connect to future lessons.</p>
        </td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student self-reflection prompt:</b></p>
            <ul>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">1. "How confident do I feel when listening to information about environmental issues and giving advice?"</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">2. "What part of listening or responding in English did I find most challenging today, and why?"</li>
            </ul>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher reflection:</b></p>
            <ul>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What worked well in this listening-focused lesson? (e.g., student engagement with audio, use of CCQs, effectiveness of group discussions).</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What adjustments or scaffolds are needed for the next lesson or future listening activities? (e.g., clearer audio, more visual support for vocabulary, more structured sentence frames for responses).</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Did students successfully infer implicit information and respond in discussions? Were the pronunciation points clear?</li>
            </ul>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson in the theme sequence:</b></p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">"In our next lesson (Lesson 2: Reading Comprehension), we will read a short text about 'The Environment and Recycling' that uses many of the same vocabulary words (<i>waste, save, recycle, environment, pollution, planet</i>) and concepts we discussed today. You'll be able to see these words in context and deepen your understanding of how 'You Can Save Water By...' reading about it."</p>
        </td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">___ min</p>
        </td>
    </tr>
</table>